<!--
  Clock quality - how far the node may trust the time it stamps with.

  A node stamps data with a time read from a clock (`wz_session_core::epoch::EpochSource`). The
  reading says WHAT time it is; this machine says HOW MUCH that reading is worth, so a node that
  must not stamp with a time it does not trust can say so (`clock-quality-stamping`), and a
  consumer of the node's observations can tell a synchronised clock from a drifting one. The
  design is `docs/observation-plane.md` M1.

  Division of labour with the host (the machine owns the states, the host owns the clock):
    - Whether the clock is synchronised, and when it stopped being, is the host's knowledge:
      NTP/PTP daemon state, a GNSS lock, a fieldbus time master. The host raises
      `sync.acquired` and `sync.lost`; this machine never reads a clock.
    - How long a lost reference may be bridged by the local oscillator (the holdover bound) is
      host arithmetic over the host's clock. The host raises `holdover.expired`, as the host
      raises every deadline of the library (a machine holds no timer of its own).
    - The uncertainty of one reading, in microseconds, is DATA OF THE READING and not a state: it
      grows continuously while a clock is held over, and a state per value is not a machine.

  The states:
    Unsynchronized - no reference has been acquired since the node started. A clock that is set
                     by hand, or that nothing disciplines, is here, and stamping from it is the
                     node's default behaviour.
    Synchronized   - a reference is acquired and the clock follows it.
    Holdover       - the reference was lost recently; the clock runs on its own oscillator and
                     is still good within the host's bound. Present only with `clock-source`.
    FreeRunning    - the holdover bound has passed with no reference back; the reading is a
                     time but not a trusted one. Present only with `clock-source`.

  Without `clock-source` the clock is the system clock, which the operating system disciplines
  and which has no holdover the node could bound, so losing the reference returns to
  Unsynchronized. With it, a clock the application registered can report the two states between.

  Engine-free: every effect is a no-arg `<sce:action>` host-trait call, so the generated
  `ClockQualityPolicy<A>` carries no `IScriptEngine` and compiles `no_std`.

  Host action (the generated `ClockQualityActions` trait):
    quality_changed - entered a state: the host re-reads the quality, moves its stamping gate and
                      counts the change.

  Events fed by the host:
    sync.acquired    - a reference is acquired (first time, or back after a loss).
    sync.lost        - the reference is gone.
    holdover.expired - the holdover bound passed with no reference back.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://sce.dev/ext sce-forge.xsd"
       sce:kind="statechart"
       name="clock_quality"
       version="1.0"
       initial="Unsynchronized">

  <sce:import kind="feature-model" src="../features/wz-session-core.scxml" as="features"/>

  <state id="Unsynchronized">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="quality_changed" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="sync.acquired" target="Synchronized"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Synchronized">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="quality_changed" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- A gated transition placed before the ungated one on the same event wins where it holds. -->
    <transition event="sync.lost" target="Holdover" sce:requires="clock-source"/>
    <transition event="sync.lost" target="Unsynchronized"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Holdover" sce:requires="clock-source">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="quality_changed" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="sync.acquired" target="Synchronized"/>
    <transition event="holdover.expired" target="FreeRunning"/>
  </state>

  <state id="FreeRunning" sce:requires="clock-source">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="quality_changed" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="sync.acquired" target="Synchronized"/>
  </state>

</scxml>
